abstract: >-
  When a person tells a language model two things about themselves
  rather than one, does the model treat them worse than the sum of the
  parts? Before that can be answered, the instrument has to be shown to
  measure people rather than words. Alongside every audited pair this
  study stacks pairs of socially neutral details built the same way, in
  the same slots, whose interaction should be zero.

  Part I puts two stacked signals through five outcome measures on three
  open models (Qwen2.5-7B, Llama-3.1-8B, Mistral-7B-v0.3), with clause
  count held constant. On four of the five instruments the neutral
  interaction is not zero. On the one instrument that holds a clean
  zero, whether the model solves the person's own arithmetic task, one
  interaction of twenty-four survives correction, and it is
  sub-additive, the size of the spread the same measurement shows across
  six neutral wrappers.

  Part II runs a neutral comparison on published work with the original
  authors' own code, under a second preregistration that requires one of
  their numbers to be reproduced first. Two targets passed that gate,
  and in both, neutral details move the statistic well above zero.
  Part III lists the five artifacts a rerun of this kind turns out to
  depend on.

  Two preregistrations, their dated addenda, all datasets, raw model
  outputs, analysis scripts and offline validators are included. Every
  reported number regenerates without a GPU, and Appendix A lists all 98
  tests behind the central table.
